/**
 * Combines two ability constraints on one generic parameter into a single requirement.
 *
 * `&` is the combinator of the `~` constraint syntax: `[A ~ Show & Eq]` binds a type that
 * has both `Show` and `Eq`. It is declared here, in the standard library, rather than being a
 * symbol the parser recognises — so the name must be in scope for `~` to accept it, and a module
 * that declares its own `&` takes it back like any other name.
 *
 * It is written between two abilities, never applied. An ability that already implies another says
 * so on its own declaration instead, and callers then need name only the one — that is the ordinary
 * superability relation, not a use of `&`.
 *
 * ```eliot
 * // A type that can be both shown and compared. `Printable` has no methods: it adds nothing
 * // itself, it only names the pair.
 * ability Printable[A ~ Show & Eq]
 *
 * // Naming the set is enough — `~ Printable` requires `Show` and `Eq` along with it.
 * def describe[A ~ Printable](a: A, b: A): String
 *
 * // The same requirement spelled directly on a generic parameter, without naming it.
 * def report[A ~ Show & Eq](a: A, b: A): String
 * ```
 */
infix left type &[A, B]
